    "overview": "<p>The Capitol System Defense Station is a dedicated orbital defense platform introduced in 3051 by Federated-Boeing Interstellar. Developed in response to the Clan Invasion's demand for rapidly deployable defensive infrastructure, the Capitol was designed to engage DropShip-class targets and larger warships with a purpose-built anti-warship weapons suite. Its compact size and relatively light mass were deliberate choices intended to give system commanders flexibility in positioning and, ultimately, the ability to redeploy the station between star systems as threats evolved. An integral aerospace fighter complement supports local space defense operations alongside its primary weapons. While the design never fully realized its strategic mobility potential, the Capitol represented an innovative attempt to combine heavy orbital firepower with operational flexibility during one of the most critical periods in Inner Sphere history.</p>",
    "capabilities": "<p>The Capitol mounts an identical weapons suite across each of its six firing arcs, ensuring consistent firepower in every direction without defensive blind spots. Each arc carries a Naval Laser 35 and a Naval Autocannon/10 as primary anti-warship batteries, capable of destroying DropShip-class targets within minutes of sustained engagement. Supporting these heavy naval weapons are paired ER Large Lasers, two standard Large Lasers, and two Medium Lasers for layered coverage across varying engagement ranges. Medium-range firepower per arc is further augmented by a Gauss Rifle and a conventional Autocannon/10. Long-range area denial is provided by Artemis IV-equipped LRM-20 and LRM-15 launchers, while a pair of Artemis IV-equipped SRM-6 launchers handles close-range defense. Three hundred thirty-nine heat sinks support sustained operation of this extensive weapons array across all arcs simultaneously.</p><p>The station accommodates twenty-four aerospace fighters organized into four squadrons, housed in dedicated bays with three access doors, along with seven small craft in a single bay with one door. Two grav decks, each 185 meters in diameter, maintain crew habitability during extended deployments. Approximately 5,742 tons of cargo space allows limited independent logistics support. The Capitol's primary operational strength lies in the combination of its compact mass and small cross-section relative to its firepower, characteristics that enable tug repositioning and, theoretically, system-to-system transit aboard a suitably modified JumpShip using a specialized adapter. However, its four-squadron fighter complement leaves it particularly more vulnerable to dedicated aerospace assault than heavier platforms such as the Bastion, a limitation acknowledged at the time of its introduction.</p>",
    "deployment": "<p>The Capitol (3051) serves as an orbital defense installation at priority systems requiring rapid-response firepower against warship and DropShip-class threats. Produced at Federated-Boeing Interstellar's Port Simon Shipyards on Galax for Federated Commonwealth system defense, it functions in practice as a fixed orbital fortification. While the design included provisions for JumpShip-transported redeployment between star systems, no vessels were equipped with the necessary adapter systems during the station's production run, and individual Capitols were deployed as conventional orbital platforms at high-value worlds. Commanders employing the Capitol benefit most from pairing it with additional aerospace fighter cover to compensate for its acknowledged vulnerability to concentrated fighter assault, and from positioning it to maximize coverage of critical orbital approach vectors rather than relying solely on its organic four-squadron wing.</p>",
    "history": "<p>Federated-Boeing Interstellar developed the Capitol class at its Port Simon Shipyards on Galax, with the design entering service in 3051 as the Inner Sphere reeled from the shock of the Clan Invasion. The urgent need for deployable orbital defense infrastructure (platforms that could be positioned wherever the conflict demanded rather than anchored permanently to individual worlds) drove the design philosophy from the outset. Federated-Boeing's established presence at Galax, a major aerospace construction center within the Federated Commonwealth, provided the industrial and technical foundation for the project.</p><p>A distinctive feature of the Capitol's original specification was its intended strategic mobility. By keeping the station's mass and overall diameter well below those of conventional defense platforms, the designers envisioned a system that could transit between star systems aboard a JumpShip carrying a specially designed adapter, with a tug managing final approach and orbital positioning at the destination. This would have made the Capitol a genuinely flexible asset, capable of rapid redeployment to any threatened system regardless of existing fixed infrastructure. In practice, no vessels were modified with the necessary adapter equipment at the time of the station's introduction, and the mobile-deployment concept remained an unrealized operational goal throughout the platform's production run.</p><p>Production at Port Simon Shipyards continued through the Clan Invasion and Civil War eras under Federated Commonwealth ownership. When the Commonwealth dissolved, the Galax facility returned to Federated Suns control, with production continuing into the Civil War period before ceasing. The Capitol class saw no renewed production during the Jihad or subsequent periods, leaving its total output modest relative to the strategic demands that had originally motivated its development.</p>",
    "manufacturer": "Federated-Boeing Interstellar",
    "primaryFactory": "Galax (Port Simon Shipyards)"
  }
}